Author a Crucible fuzz-harness FIXTURE (only) for the Solana program `{{ program }}`.

The fixture is the ONLY thing that can drive the program: the invariant tests written against it may
read state but never send instructions (an invariant that mutates state corrupts the campaign). So
the fixture's `action_*` methods and its initial state decide which properties can be checked at all
— everything you leave out is a property that can only be "checked" by an assertion that is true no
matter what the program does. Design it for these {{ n }} properties:
{{ listed }}

Concretely, for that list:
- **One `action_*` per instruction those properties exercise.** An instruction with no action is
  unreachable state the fuzzer can never explore.
- **Negative attempts are actions too.** For a property of the form "X must be rejected" (re-init,
  an unauthorized caller, a flash borrow with no repay, an operation while paused), write an action
  that *attempts* X and asserts it failed — `fuzz_assert!(res.is_err(), "[<property title>] …")`. A
  rejected call changes nothing, so this is safe inside an action. It cannot go in the test.
  **Such an action must `return true`.** The bool reports whether the ACTION did what it was designed
  to do — not whether the instruction succeeded — and for this one the rejection *is* it working.
  Returning `false` costs you twice: Crucible reads `false` as a dead-end state and **ends the action
  sequence there**, so every draw of this action truncates the campaign; and a violation reported on
  a `false` action is auto-labelled a suspected harness bug, which is precisely backwards for an
  action whose entire purpose is the rejection.
- **Enough actors and accounts to make the property non-degenerate.** A property about "only the
  owner may X" needs a second, non-owner signer; "at most N positions" needs enough reserves/markets
  to reach N; a per-user property needs two users. One of each is a fixture that proves nothing.
- **Realistic configuration.** Never set a limit, cap or threshold to `u64::MAX` (or a fee to 0) just
  to keep setup simple: it makes the property about that limit vacuously true. Pick values the fuzzer
  can actually cross.
- **Expose typed reads.** Give the tests helper methods returning the state they must assert on
  (`pub fn reserve(&self) -> State`, a scaled-fraction → integer helper, PDA accessors), so a test
  never has to parse raw account bytes at hand-computed offsets.

State the fixture holds must be reachable from the tests: make the fields the properties talk about
`pub(crate)` or expose an accessor.
